CHANGE ANALYSIS (citations verified, indeterminate)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────
Cannot be established from this repository alone: depends on state only known at runtime (e.g. feature flags, request data).
The custom-port mechanism is explicit, but its real observable effect depends on runtime configuration selecting a non-default value, which browser setup branch is entered, and the live state of that localhost port and Chrome process. The supplied repository slice does not show whether those conditions occur in an actual installation.

Browser startup now supports a configurable Chrome remote-debugging port, defaulting to the existing Chrome debug-port constant. The selected port is used when probing for an existing browser, connecting over CDP, constructing launch arguments, and checking port occupancy. User-provided Chrome binaries are now launched through direct argv-based subprocess execution. The agent and file-upload edits appear non-semantic, while the new test targets custom-port startup.

Likely behavioral changes
  • Browser callers can select a Chrome remote-debugging port. For a user-provided browser binary, the browser setup probes and connects to that selected localhost port and supplies the same port when launching Chrome; built-in browser launch also constructs its debugging argument from the selected value rather than a fixed port.
  • When starting a user-supplied Chrome executable, browser setup now invokes the configured binary directly and passes Chrome options as separate process arguments, rather than routing the launch through the previous subprocess-shell call shape.

Investigate
  • Trace all BrowserConfig construction, serialization, aliases, and wrappers to determine where custom or null debugging-port values can enter and whether validation is imposed elsewhere.
  • Follow browser selection and lifecycle control to establish when the user-provided path versus built-in path runs, and how process ownership and cleanup behave when an existing browser is reused.
  • Inspect Chrome and Playwright assumptions around debugging-port arguments, duplicate flags, occupied ports, readiness polling, and CDP connection behavior.

Uncertain
  • The supplied context does not show which production callers construct BrowserConfig with a custom or explicitly null chrome_remote_debugging_port, or which callers enter each browser setup branch.
  • No HTTP endpoint is shown, so it cannot be established whether any route exposes this browser configuration or invokes the changed setup paths.
  • Whether setup reuses an existing browser, launches a new process, or removes the built-in debugging argument depends on whether the selected localhost port is occupied and answers the Chrome version probe at runtime.
  • The diff does not establish Chrome's precedence for multiple remote-debugging-port arguments or Playwright's behavior when the explicit debugging argument is removed because the configured port is occupied.
  • The ownership and lifecycle boundary for a browser discovered on the configured localhost port is not shown, including whether it is always safe for this Browser instance to reuse it or later close related resources.

